- F-231 **The generated AST walker is split into eight methods that the JVM compiles; as one method it ran interpreted and took most of the checker's time.** `Walker.walk`, which `ScalaAstGenerator` writes into `FortressAst.scala`, was one method of 31,704 bytes, past HotSpot's 8,000-byte limit for compiling a method, and the checker's substitutions and normalizations all go through it. The split, in order and with identical output, was measured on climb batch 15's code with the repair that lets the checker check a block's local functions (row 680; F-233), the two changes that climb batch 15b then landed, on an idle machine. It took the count stage from 911 s to about 340 s and the distance stage from 2,375 s to 931 s; every run had the repair. In the library component's type check, about 95 % of the time that remained was the solver reducing formulas again, which its memos now answer (F-235). F-240 gives the stages' times now. Gated by `WalkerJUTest`, which fails if a method of `Walker` passes 8,000 bytes and checks the split walker's answers. Detail: `perf-probes/checker-speed-2/REPORT.md` sections 2 and 6.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f8/proposals.md, "F-231"); the times bounded to climb batch 15b's code (reviews/batch-16-review.md section 2); row 680 given its subject (F-233); moved here, as detail of the solver's memos that its report holds: with the reduction memo alone the count stage's profile held the exclusion questions (pExc, dExc) at about 62 to 65 % of a run of 110 s against the base's 368 s, and the final code has no profile (compile-ladder/rung-solver-speed/REPORT.md section 5); after the check: the code measured said as climb batch 15's with row 680's line, the two changes that 15b landed (perf-probes/checker-speed-2/REPORT.md opening comment; climb-batch-15b/RECORD.md, "What landed"), every run with the repair (section 6); the 95 % bounded to the library component's type check (section 2.3, :391); "set the checker's time" said as most of it (section 2.2); WalkerJUTest said by what it checks
- F-232 **The syntax tree's hashes do not depend on the JVM's identity hashes, except at inference variables, walk's wrapped values and the nodes that hold them.** Every generated node's hash starts from the hash of its class's name: makeAST's post-processing of ASTGen's output writes that start, and so do the in-repo generators (`build.xml:679-697`). A field whose type holds an `Option` is hashed by `NodeHash.of` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/nodes_util/NodeHash.java`), since the PLT `Option` classes hash by identity. So hash maps and sets of nodes that hold neither exception iterate in one order whatever code asked for an identity hash before, and so do the apis that `StaticChecker.checkApis` takes in turn. The exceptions hash through identity hashes: an inference variable's id is a bare `Object` (`nodes_util/NodeFactory.java:1166-1184`), but no output of the count stage moves through its hash; walk's `_WrappedFValue` hashes its `FValue` by walk's own hash, which can be an identity hash (`interpreter/evaluator/_WrappedFValue.java:170-177`; row 430). `DeclaredFunction`s, entries of the checker's index and not nodes, still hash by identity, but since row 737's repair no choice of `ApiTypeExtractor` depends on their order (F-254). Gated by `NodeHashJUTest`, and row 737's repair by `OmittedReturnTypeFromApi`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-ast-hashes/REPORT.md` sections 4.2 to 4.4.
  by: the clarity pass; note: the title limited to the exceptions that the body names (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f8/proposals.md, "F-232"); the two exceptions each said in their own clause; after the check: why each exception is one said, an FValue's hash able to be an identity hash (row 430; rung-ast-hashes/REPORT.md section 1); DeclaredFunction said as an entry of the checker's index, not a node (section 4.3); the verb of the generators' sentence placed by its object
- F-237 **The well-formedness walk reports an ill-formed type whose span lies outside the expression that the walk is in at that expression, so a call whose static argument breaks the bound of the callee's result type is reported at the call.** A call's type keeps the spans of the callee's declared type. An error on such a type is reported at the innermost expression the walk met it in, once for each call that made the type: the expressions that a call's value flows to share its type node and add no error, and a second call with the same printed static argument gets its own error. One known gap, still open (row 776): of two nested calls of one callee at static arguments that print alike, `next[\T\](next[\T\](x))`, only the inner call is reported; the program is still refused. Where the type is ill-formed where it is written, its error stays at the declaration. A local variable's right-hand side is walked before the lines that use the variable, so a call whose value a variable holds is reported at the call, not at a later use. In the library, by reading, reporting at the call adds and drops no error against reporting at the declaration, and the distance holds no well-formedness error (F-53). Source: `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/scala_src/typechecker/TypeWellFormedChecker.scala`, `check`, `error`, `outside` and `within`, and its `SLocalVarDecl` case. Gated by `compiler_tests/XXXStaticArgBoundAtCall`, `XXXStaticArgBoundAtCallOnce`, `XXXStaticArgBoundAtEachCall`, `XXXStaticArgBoundAtCallNotAtUse` and `XXXIllFormedTypeAtDeclarationOnce`, and `XXXStaticArgBoundNestedCall`, which pins row 776's single error. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-checker-abstract-callsite/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 6, its `SKEPTIC.md` sections 5.1 and 5.2 and its `JUDGE.md`.
  by: the clarity pass; note: row 776's exception added beside "once for each call" (TypeWellFormedChecker.scala:50-56; XXXStaticArgBoundNestedCall.test), with its test (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f8/proposals.md, "F-237"); the source lines :37-114 and :284-287, the second drifted, replaced by the functions' names and the SLocalVarDecl case; after the check: row 776 said as an open gap, not part of the rule, its test said to pin today's single error (row 776, implementation gap); the title's "it" said as the type's span; the baseline of "added or dropped" said as reporting at the declaration (JUDGE.md:81)
- F-240 **The checker's count stage takes about 63 s and its distance stage 161 s at the last landed gate, climb batch 20's.** At climb batch 15b's gate they took 325 s and 1,075 s; the solver's memos (F-235) make most of the difference, not load. At climb batch 20's gate the count stage ran alone after the ladder, and the distance stage beside `testFast`; the same library text and checker took 139 s for the distance stage on an idle machine. Load can lengthen a stage more than twofold: climb batch 16's merged code took 217 s for the distance stage at its gate, at a load average of 3.3, and nearly the same code took 574 s at a load average of 4.6 to 5.3. So a time compares only with one taken under a like load. Source: the gate's result and `compile-ladder/climb-batch-20/gate/distance.txt`. Detail: `reviews/batch-20-review.md`, section 2; `reviews/batch-17-review.md`, section 1; `reviews/batch-16-review.md`, section 2.
  by: the clarity pass; note: "rung S's tree took 574 s ... at load 5" said as climb batch 16's merged code, 217 s at its gate at load 3.32, against nearly the same code, climb batch 16's rung S (solver speed), 574 s at load 4.6 to 5.3 (reviews/batch-16-review.md section 2) (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f8/proposals.md, "F-240"); after the check: "make the difference" said as most of it, since the later fall to 63 s and 161 s is put down to load and the machine (reviews/batch-18-review.md:472; batch-20-review.md section 2); the idle run said to use the same checker; load said as the load average
- F-242 **`SUM`'s fusion pairs with `MAX` and `MIN` hold no site of the distance: a `where` condition on each of `SumReduction`'s two `distribute` overloads clears their four unmet bounds, and `ActualReduction`'s generic `distribute`, which `SumReduction` inherits, with its result over its own `K`, clears their two return-type errors.** The fusion pairs are `MaxSumReductionPair`, `MinSumReductionPair` and those two overloads, through which the team's `Generator2` computes the maximum or the minimum of sums in one pass. The two errors were at the two overloads, which answer a pair over `Maybe[\T\]` beside the inherited `distribute`. With the inherited one answering `PossibleReductionPair[\K\]`, they pass the return-type rule (F-75). The team's commented text, which answers `PossibleReductionPair[\R\]`, only moves the two errors to the generic. The fusion stays (P-151). Row 433, fixed; F-250 gives the landing and its test. Detail: `perf-probes/where-clauses/REPORT.md` A.1 and A.2.1; `perf-probes/generic-distribute/REPORT.md` sections 3 and 4.
